Characterization of dsDNA-Capped MOFs. To verify the formation of dsDNA-capped MOFs, X-ray diffraction (XRD) technique was conducted. As shown in Figure 1A, the synthesized UIO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , MB@UIO, and TMB@UIO displayed sharp diffraction peaks in XRD patterns, which are highly consistent with the simulated data from single crystal, implying the good purity and high crystallinity. No peaks from
MB or TMB molecules were determined, suggesting that no MB/TMB crystals existed in MB@UIO/TMB@UIO materials. Further, transmission electron microscopy (TEM) analysis was applied to investigate their morphologies (Figure 1B-D). Identical diameter $55\mathrm{nm}$ ) was observed for UIO-66-NH2, MB@UIO, and TMB@UIO, respectively. This indicated that MB or TMB made little influence on the morphologies of the prepared MOFs. To gain more insights into dsDNA-capped MOFs, scanning electron microscope (SEM), energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), and energy dispersive X-ray spectrometer (EDX) were conducted to verify the DNA effectively assembling as gatekeeper (Figure S1-3), and FT-IR spectra were carried out to confirm the electroactive dyes effectively encapsulated (Figure 1E). Evidently, new peaks including 1599, 1180, 1142, 1034, 950, and $885~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ appeared in MB $@$ UIO compared with that in UIO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , justifying MB effectively encapsulated in $\mathrm{UIO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The same conclusion can also be obtained from that of TMB@UIO (Figure S4). Meanwhile, new element $\mathrm{\bfP}$ appeared in TMB $@$ UIO compared with that in UIO- $66\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and was assigned to the DNA, suggesting that DNA was linked on the surface of UIO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ through the amide reaction and nucleic acid hybridization to form the biogate. Furthermore, zeta potential was applied to study the surface functionalization of the prepared MOFs (Figure 1F). UIO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ were electropositive and the value was determined to be $+21.67\mathrm{mV}$ . As expected, the potential became to $-37.64~\mathrm{mV}/-38.24~\mathrm{mV}$ after the linkage of ssDNA, and the hybridizing with $\mathrm{P_{X}}$ would further increase the negative charge.
Prior to application of dsDNA-capped MOFs in analysis of miRNAs, their stability was evaluated through dispersing them in PBS buffer for different time or in human serum sample. As shown in Figure S5, the MB@UIO and TMB@UIO were stable in the absence of target analytes, and thus no significant current change was observed. More importantly, their currents appeared negligible change too in human serum sample even the time was extended to be $^\textrm{\scriptsize7h}$ . These results indicated that dsDNA could be used as gatekeeper to prevent the electroactive dyes release and the functionalized MOFs enjoyed excellent stability. Exonuclease III (Exo III), capable of catalyzing the stepwise removal of mononucleotides from 3'-OH termini of dsDNA, was applied to further evaluate the influence of DNA structures on encapsulating efficiency. The interaction between Exo III and dsDNA-capped MOFs was illustrated in Figure 2A. Evidently, only a small amount of ssDNA existed on MOFs surface after the Exo III-mediated cleavage reaction, subsequently leading to a large amount of electroactive dyes release into the solution and two strong electrochemical signals (Figure 2B). The reason for this phenomenon is due to the disappearance of dsDNA capped on MOFs surface.
Feasibility Study of let-7a and miRNA-21 Simultaneous Detection. To confirm the feasibility of the proposed biosensor for let-7a and miRNA-21 simultaneous detection, the DPV signals of MB $\mathrm{(I_{MB})}$ and TMB $\mathrm{(I_{TMB})}$ with different concentrations were recorded. As shown in Figure 3A, MB and TMB have different potentials located at $_{-0.21\mathrm{~V~}}$ and $0.32{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the DPV signals increased with the increase of their concentrations, justifying the possibility of MB and TMB utilized as electroactive dyes in development of homogeneous electrochemical biosensor for simultaneous detection of let-7a and miRNA-21. Subsequently, the electrochemical signals of the proposed biosensor in the absence/presence of target analytes were determined (Figure 3B). If no target analytes existed in the sensing system, the oxidation currents of MB and TMB were 55.51 nA and $38.58\mathrm{\nA}$ , respectively.
Whereas, only in the presence of let-7a, the oxidation current of MB increased to 134.73 nA, accompanied with that of TMB remained slightly change. In contrast, if only miRNA-21 existed, the oxidation current of TMB increased to 137.00 nA, accompanied with that of MB remained slightly change. This is because the effective release of electroactive dyes is dependent on the target-initiated hybridization reaction. Thus, when both of let-7a and miRNA-21 existed, the oxidation currents of MB and TMB increased to 132.07 nA and 134.73 nA, respectively. These above information obviously indicate that the MOFs-based homogeneous electrochemical strategy can achieve let-7a and miRNA-21 simultaneous detection.
