To further verify the feasibility of the proposed strategy, fluorescence characterizations were carried out and the results were illustrated in Figure 3C-F. TMB and MB have different emission wavelength of 398.65 nm and $683.60\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. Compared with the fluorescence intensities of MB@UIO and TMB@UIO, both stronger signals were obtained in the presence of let-7a and miRNA-21, with the intensities located at 320.19 a. u. and 360.45 a. u., respectively. The significantly increased FL intensities are indubitably ascribed to the efficient release of MB and TMB from MOFs through the targets-induced hybridization reaction. Furthermore, when only one target analyte is present, there is only one stronger fluorescence signal recorded. From this context, it can be inferred that the proposed biosensor met the demand for let-7a and miRNA-21 simultaneous detection through the fluorescence intensity variation. However, it should be noted that there are some problems for simultaneous fluorescence biosensing. 1) MB, TMB, and other fluorescence dyes used for simultaneous biosensing have different UV-vis spectra and fluorescence spectra, which might result in fluorescence resonance energy transfer and mutual interference, thus reducing the diagnosis accuracy. 2) In general, they have different excitation wavelengths, making the simultaneous biosensing disadvantageous in one single assay. 3) In addition, most of the fluorescence strategies suffered from the complex centrifugation procedure. By contrast, MOFs-based homogeneous electrochemical biosensor can successfully avoid these drawbacks for let-7a and miRNA-21 simultaneous detection.
Optimization of Experimental Conditions. Prior to simultaneous detection of let-7a and miRNA-21 using our established platform, the amount of electroactive dyes and $\mathrm{\DeltaP_{X}}$ , and the hybridization time between target analytes and dsDNA-capped MOFs were optimized to get the best performance. First, since the amount of electroactive dyes encapsulated in UIO-66- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ made significant influence on DPV signals, and thus was studied. From Figure S6A, the DPV current increased with MB concentration increasing, implying that more MB were entrapped in UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ . When MB concentration increased to $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , the DPV signal got the maximum value. Thus, $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ was served as the optimal MB concentration to improve the sensing sensitivity. Next, the amount of $\mathrm{\bfP_{\mathrm{MB}}}$ for capping MB in the pores of UIO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ was investigated. As depicted in Figure S6C, with the concentration of $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{MB}}$ increased from 100 to $1000~\mathrm{{nM}}$ , the DPV currents increased in the initial stage and leveled off when the amount reached $600~\mathrm{{nM}}$ . Thus, $600~\mathrm{{nM}}$ was served as the optimum concentration. Finally, the hybridization time between let-7a and MB@UIO was also monitored. As shown in Figure S6E, the electrochemical signal elevated at a high speed initially, and reached a plateau at $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ , suggesting $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ is sufficient for the hybridization reaction between let-7a and MB $@$ UIO. For TMB-participated biosensor, the amounts of TMB and $\mathbf{P}_{\mathrm{TMB}}$ , and the hybridization time were optimized to be $100~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ , $600{\mathrm{nM}}$ , and $60\mathrm{min}$ , respectively.
Simultaneous Detection of let-7a and miRNA-21. To confirm the ability of MB@UIO and TMB@UIO to simultaneously detect let-7a and miRNA-21, a series of experiments were conducted under the optimized conditions. Figure 4A gave the electrochemical responses determined upon the addition of let-7a and miRNA-21 with different concentrations. Compared with that of the blank sample (MB@UIO and TMB@UIO), the DPV currents increased obviously with both of let-7a and miRNA-21 concentrations increasing, which could be attributable to the sensing principle that more target analytes triggered the release of more electroactive dyes (MB and TMB), thus enhancing the DPV currents. Standard working curves made by plotting DPV currents versus the let-7a and miRNA-21 concentrations were depicted in Figure 4B and 4C. The $\mathrm{I_{MB}}$ and $\mathrm{I_{TMB}}$ were found to be linearly relevant to the logarithm let-7a and miRNA-21 concentrations $\mathrm{(lgC_{let-7a}}$ and $\mathrm{lgC}_{\mathrm{miRNA-21},}$ in the range of $0.01{\-}10\mathrm{\pM}$ and $0.02\mathrm{-}10\textrm{}\mathrm{pM}.$ , respectively. For let-7a analysis, the regression equation was determined as $\mathrm{I_{MB}{=}32.4017l g C_{l e t-7a}}\ +$ 118.5219 with coefficient $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ of 0.9824 and detection limit (LOD) of $3.6~\mathrm{fM}$ based on $3\upsigma$ ; for miRNA-21 analysis, the regression equation was $\mathrm{I_{TMB}}{=}38.3024\mathrm{lg}\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{miRNA-21}}{+}117.0456$ with $\mathrm{R}^{2}$ of 0.9917 and LOD of 8.2 fM based on $3\upsigma$ . The LOD, as shown in Table S2, was comparable or superior to those of the reported methods that concentrated on single miRNA detection and suffered from complicated and expensive signal molecule-participated labeling procedure, enzyme-assisted signal amplification strategy, and long responsive time. This high sensitivity may be originated from the UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ’s highly porous structure permitting for encapsulating a large number of electroactive dyes, and the superior gating effect of dsDNA to ssDNA allowing the more amount of electroactive dyes release in the presence of only a small amount of target analytes. Overall, this MOFs-based electrochemical biosensor manifests evident advantages of simple operation, rapid response, and low cost, and can become a more powerful tool for the simultaneous and ultrasensitive detection of multiple miRNAs, benefiting the accurate and early cancer diagnosis.
